<%#
  Split auth shell: brand panel on the left at `lg`, form on the right.
  No skip link -- there is no repeated navigation block ahead of the content to bypass.
%>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="<%= I18n.locale %>">
<head>
  <%= render "layouts/essentials_head" %>
</head>
<body class="min-h-screen bg-white font-sans text-slate-900 antialiased">
  <div class="flex min-h-screen">
    <%#
      A <header> rather than a <div>: at body level it is the banner landmark, so this branding
      is inside a landmark like the rest of the page. axe reports content outside landmarks, and
      it was right -- this was the only orphan.

      Hiding it from assistive technology instead would have been easier and worse: it is real
      content, and a sighted user on a wide screen gets it.
    %>
    <%#
      Pinned to the viewport (`sticky top-0 h-screen`), not stretched to the page. As a stretched
      flex item it grew with the form beside it, and `justify-between` slid its middle block down
      the page: "Human Essentials" went from y=362 to y=497 when the account request form opened.
    %>
    <header class="hidden w-1/2 flex-col justify-between bg-brand-700 p-12 text-white lg:sticky lg:top-0 lg:flex lg:h-screen">
      <img src="/img/essentials.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" class="h-10 w-auto">
      <div>
        <p class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Human Essentials</p>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-md text-base text-brand-100">
          Inventory, distributions and partner management for diaper banks and essentials banks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-brand-200">Built by Ruby for Good.</p>
    </header>

    <%# Centred, except on a page whose content grows when it is answered -- `:auth_align_top` --
        where centring would move everything above the part that grew. %>
    <%#
      The page is a card on slate-50, on every auth screen -- chosen from the bank finder preview
      (docs/mockups/bank-finder-designs.html). The card is here, once, so no view draws its own:
      a view that renders `shared/essentials/card` inside it is a card in a card. 26rem wide with
      32px padding is the preview's frame, and leaves a 352px column; 24px padding below `sm`,
      so a 320px phone still has 240px of it.
    %>
    <main id="main-content" class="flex w-full flex-col <%= content_for?(:auth_align_top) ? "justify-start" : "justify-center" %> bg-slate-50 px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:w-1/2 lg:px-16">
      <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-104">
        <img src="/img/essentials.svg" alt="Human Essentials" class="mb-8 h-10 w-auto lg:hidden">
        <div class="card-surface p-6 sm:p-8">

        <%#
          Staging warning. The AdminLTE version was a Bootstrap `.modal fade` that nothing ever
          opened -- there was no JS anywhere in the repo referencing it -- plus a checkbox that
          gated a permanently `disabled` button. It only "worked" because the modal CSS was not
          loaded on that layout, so it rendered as plain visible text.

          A warning nobody can dismiss should not pretend to be dismissable: this is a callout,
          always visible on the page it warns about.
        %>
        <% if Rails.env.staging? %>
          <%= render "shared/essentials/callout", tone: :warning, wrapper_class: "mb-6", callout_id: "staging-warning" do %>
            <p class="flex items-start gap-2 font-semibold">
            This site is for testing only.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-2">
            You are on <%= request.host %>, a demo site for
            <%= link_to production_host, production_url, class: "font-medium underline" %>.
            It is not safe to upload, enter or save any sensitive data here.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-2">
            To sign in to your live account, go to
            <%= link_to "#{production_host}/users/sign_in", production_url("/users/sign_in"), class: "font-medium underline" %>.
            </p>

          <% end %>
        <% end %>

        <%= render "shared/essentials/flash" %>
        <%= yield %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </main>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
